module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.PurelyInseparable.Basic

/-!
# Finite coefficient-root extensions of rational function fields

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 04KM, the coefficient-root construction.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-coefficient-root-extension)
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false
universe w
open Polynomial
namespace FieldExtension

/-- A polynomial over a finitely generated rational function field acquires p-th-power
coefficients after extending the constants and rooting an algebraically independent tuple.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 04KM, the construction of k' and L.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-coefficient-root-extension)

Proof. Work in an algebraic closure Ω of K. Write E = k(s). Every coefficient c of P
is f(s)/g(s), with f,g multivariate polynomials over k and g(s) nonzero: such quotients
form a subfield containing k and s and hence all of E. Put h = f g^(p-1). Take the
finite union C of the coefficients of these h for the finitely many nonzero coefficients
of P. Let l be the subfield of Ω generated over k by the unique p-th roots of C, and
let t consist of the unique p-th roots of s. Let L be the compositum of K and l(t)
in Ω. These are actual subfields, in the universe of K. Frobenius is a ring homomorphism
and injective in Ω. The subfield of elements whose p-th powers lie in k contains l;
likewise the subfield of elements whose p-th powers lie in K contains L. Thus l/k
and L/K are purely inseparable. Each is generated by finitely many roots of polynomials
X^p-c, so each is finite. The original E embeds in E₁=l(t), since s_i=t_i^p; these
inclusions give the asserted algebra structure and scalar tower. For h=Σ c_I X^I,
put h̃=Σ c_I^(1/p) t^I in E₁. Then h̃^p=h(s), and c=(h̃/g(s))^p, proving the coefficient
claim, also for zero coefficients. A polynomial relation over l among t, raised to the
p-th power, gives a relation over k among s. The root map identifies s bijectively
with t, so the variables are reindexed by a bijection. Coefficientwise Frobenius is injective,
so a nonzero relation stays nonzero; this contradicts algebraic independence of s.
By construction K and E₁ generate L. When K/E is finite, the compositum degree
bound gives finiteness of L/E₁ and the transcendence-basis property. The caller
proves these and finite generation; no degree estimate is asserted here. -/
theorem exists_coefficientRoot_extension
    (k K : Type w) [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (s : Finset K)
    (hs : AlgebraicIndependent k ((↑) : s → K))
    (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] [CharP k p]
    (P : (IntermediateField.adjoin k (s : Set K))[X]) :
    ∃ (l : Type w) (hl : Field l) (akl : Algebra k l)
      (L : Type w) (hL : Field L) (aKL : Algebra K L) (alL : Algebra l L)
      (akL : Algebra k L) (htl : IsScalarTower k l L) (htK : IsScalarTower k K L),
      Module.Finite k l ∧ IsPurelyInseparable k l ∧
      Module.Finite K L ∧ IsPurelyInseparable K L ∧
      ∃ t : Finset L, AlgebraicIndependent l ((↑) : t → L) ∧
        ∃ (a : Algebra (IntermediateField.adjoin k (s : Set K))
            (IntermediateField.adjoin l (t : Set L))),
          IsScalarTower (IntermediateField.adjoin k (s : Set K))
            (IntermediateField.adjoin l (t : Set L)) L ∧
          (∀ n : ℕ, ∃ b : IntermediateField.adjoin l (t : Set L),
            algebraMap (IntermediateField.adjoin k (s : Set K))
              (IntermediateField.adjoin l (t : Set L)) (P.coeff n) = b ^ p) ∧
          IntermediateField.adjoin (IntermediateField.adjoin l (t : Set L))
            (Set.range (algebraMap K L)) = ⊤ := by sorry

end FieldExtension
